<div class="qul-docs">
  <h2>About recitation data</h2>

  <p>
    Every recitation on QUL comes as audio files plus <strong>segment data</strong> — the timestamps that
    say which word is being recited at which moment. Segments are what lets you highlight words as the
    audio plays.
  </p>

  <p>Recitations come in one of two shapes:</p>

  <h3>Surah by surah (gapless)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>114 audio files, one per surah.</li>
    <li>Each file plays the whole surah without pauses between ayahs.</li>
    <li>Segments tell you where each ayah starts and ends inside that one long file.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Ayah by ayah (gapped)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>6,236 audio files, one per ayah.</li>
    <li>Each file contains a single ayah, so you can play one ayah without downloading a whole surah.</li>
    <li>Segments are relative to the start of that ayah's own file.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-info">
    A segment is a three-number array: <code>[word position, start in ms, end in ms]</code>.
    Word positions start at 1 and match the word order of the ayah.
  </div>

  <h2>Data format</h2>

  <h3>Ayah by ayah</h3>
  <p>
    The JSON file is one object keyed by ayah key (<code>surah:ayah</code>). The SQLite file has the same fields in
    the <code>verses</code> table, one row per ayah, with <code>segments</code> stored as JSON text.
  </p>
  <div class="docs-code">
    <div class="docs-code-header"><span>JSON</span></div>
    <pre><code>{
  "1:1": {
    "surah_number": 1,
    "ayah_number": 1,
    "audio_url": "https://.../001001.mp3",
    "duration": 6,
    "segments": [
      [1, 0, 1030],
      [2, 1030, 1650]
    ]
  }
}</code></pre>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><code>surah_number</code>: the surah, 1 to 114.</li>
    <li><code>ayah_number</code>: the ayah within the surah.</li>
    <li><code>audio_url</code>: the ayah's audio file.</li>
    <li><code>duration</code>: length of the file in seconds.</li>
    <li><code>segments</code>: word timings, relative to the start of the file.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Surah by surah</h3>
  <p>
    The JSON download holds two files. <code>surah.json</code> lists the audio files, keyed by surah number:
    <code>surah_number</code>, <code>audio_url</code> and <code>duration</code> (in seconds).
    <code>segments.json</code> holds the timings, keyed by <code>surah:ayah</code>. <code>timestamp_from</code> and
    <code>timestamp_to</code> locate the ayah inside the surah's audio file; the word timings inside
    <code>segments</code> use the same clock.
  </p>
  <div class="docs-code">
    <div class="docs-code-header"><span>JSON</span></div>
    <pre><code>{
  "1:1": {
    "segments": [
      [1, 361, 1051],
      [2, 1051, 1622],
      [3, 1662, 2763],
      [4, 2803, 4385]
    ],
    "duration_sec": 4,
    "duration_ms": 4224,
    "timestamp_from": 361,
    "timestamp_to": 4585
  }
}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h4>SQLite tables</h4>
  <p>
    The SQLite file has two tables: <code>surah_list</code> (<code>surah_number</code>, <code>audio_url</code>,
    <code>duration</code>) and <code>segments</code>, one row per ayah:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>surah_number</code> / <code>ayah_number</code>: the ayah.</li>
    <li><code>duration_sec</code>: length of the ayah in seconds.</li>
    <li><code>timestamp_from</code> / <code>timestamp_to</code>: start and end of the ayah in the surah's file, in ms.</li>
    <li><code>segments</code>: word timings as JSON text.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-info">
    Some surah-by-surah recitations also have letter-level timings, as a separate download with
    <code>surah_number</code>, <code>ayah_number</code> and <code>segments</code> per ayah.
  </div>
</div>
